title: 'UI: the model view and the module''s own widgets share one identity space'
updated: 2026-11-14T22:28:09Z
---

The model view's widgets and the module's own widgets share the ui view's identity space (r<rev>:<id>). A module that draws a colliding identity in an earlier frame can prime the model view's field or choice state. It stays inside the module's own view - which the shell frames as the model view anyway - so it reaches nothing new, but a module can reach into the state of the view it does not own. A per-view nonce in the identity would close it; that changes the identity scheme, so it needs a decision rather than a patch. Found by the 2026-11-14 work on k108.

2026-11-14 22:09 2026-11-14 research (canlin-scout, read-only, not executed): the root cause is that the model view and the module's own widgets are drawn into the SAME canlin_ui_view object - one slot table, one focus id, one focus-order array, one graph-pan table, one frame. The separation that exists today is accidental: it holds only because the built-in assistant module passes no explicit opts.id (src/app/assistant.c:214-218), so its widgets land in the automatic namespace and the model's in the explicit one (identity() at src/ui/ui.c:1669-1695 mixes the seed 'i' for explicit ids and 'l' for label+ordinal). A module is free to pass opts.id, and identity() accepts it from any caller - the moment the host module writes ui:field('x', v, {id = 'r5:item'}) it lands in the same 64-bit space as the model's r5:item widget. The module can aim the shot: the revision is public (canlin.view.draw() returns it) and the model's widget ids are public (the submission payload is keyed by them). Two failure modes: in the same frame the duplicate check fires loudly ('two stateful widgets with identity in one draw'), but in an EARLIER frame the priming is silent - slots_evict (src/ui/ui.c:1195-1204) keeps the slots used in the frame that just ended, so the model's widget inherits the module's slot. Primable: section open state, choice expanded state, focus, graph pan. NOT the field text - that lives in the Lua caller's variable or in the assistant's own per-slot state. The scout states plainly that it did not verify by execution: the cross-frame priming is a reading, not a measurement, and it names the regression test that would settle it. Four options with their costs: (a) a per-view nonce in the identity - one file, four spots, but INERT for this card, because both populations share one view and would share the nonce; (b) an owner-scoped namespace - the ui layer learns an owner and mixes it into the hash, six identity() call sites, canlin_ui_push carries the scope, three push sites pass it, a public header change; it separates identity but not appearance, so k114 stays; (c) a separate ui view for the model view - the assistant view gets its own view with its own slot table, focus and rect, the shell lays out two rects per pane, canlin_assistant_view_frame() is the ready-made mechanism but has no production caller; it solves both cards. The specs pull in different directions: UI §3 already thinks in namespaces and §7 scopes id-uniqueness to a view the module does not own, which points at (b); UI §4 speaks of 'module views' and 'model views' as two different things - the spec's world model already asserts the separation the code lacks - which points at (c). See the card's companion k114 and the note there for the frame half.

2026-11-14 22:28 2026-11-14, decided by the owner: the model view gets its OWN canlin_ui_view - a separate ui view with its own slot table, focus, graph state, scroll and rectangle - and the shell lays out two rectangles in the pane. This is the only option that leaves UI §4 true as written ('a view built from the GUI tool sits in a frame labelled as model-generated'), and it solves the frame half (k114) as well. The measurement that preceded the decision (canlin-test-writer, by execution in the real sandbox with the real assistant-view binding, 3321 checks green): the claim holds. A module can prime all four state kinds of the model view silently across a frame boundary - section open (src/ui/ui.c:2328-2333), choice expanded (:2113), focus (the view field v->focus, via edit_focus_sync :1942-1949) and graph pan (:2243 -> src/ui/graph.c:480-509) - while the field TEXT is not inherited, because the text is not in the slot table. The carrier is slots_evict (:1195-1204, called at :1319): the slot the module wrote in frame N survives into frame N+1, where identity() finds it and does not raise. In the SAME frame the duplicate check fires loudly ('two stateful widgets with identity in one draw') and aborts the whole draw. Three nuances the measurement added: the section's open state is latched (the module's opts.open only wins if it primes before the model ever draws that identity in that revision); the priming survives exactly one frame boundary, and stays only because the model's widget keeps the identity alive every frame; and two of the four kinds need a hit on the module's own widget, because the module has no input injection in its Lua API - section open and graph pan are reachable by drawing alone. The scope is the module's own view; the exposure is to any module the operator grants the assistant-view capability. The work is split into three cards, one per lane, and lands in the order that keeps the tree working: the shell's second view first, then the app that creates it, then the binding that draws into it.
